  {
    "episode_id": 83746,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83746.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, great, James. See, thanks for having me on, man. We just got done voting on this, got done debating. I'll tell you a story during the debate of what the Democrats were arguing for on this. I mean, against this bill or what they were saying is outrageous. But first, let me break it down. I think you did a really good job introducing it. It right now is not a federal crime to not to not yield to federal law enforcement like it is if a local police officer tries to pull you over. You know, and you just all of a sudden take off that that obviously becomes a crime. That's not the case for federal law enforcement. So you have these high speed changes happening in local neighborhoods and just area school zones that there's an endangering lives. So it obviously puts the officer's life of danger, but also innocent bystanders. We've had deaths happen in Coaches County of a woman that was on her way to her own birthday party and she got struck by a high speed chase and was killed and never made it to obviously her own birthday celebration. So you've had these cases over and over along the border. So when I first got elected, I went to the community and I said, what is the number one bill? Like the one thing, the top one, and this is what they need. I heard this from Democrat mayors, from law enforcement, from community members, everyone. So we got to work on this last Congress that passed the House with 56 Democrats actually voted for it.",
